<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anakena</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anakena is a white-sand beach on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-island">Rapa Nui</a> (Easter Island). Island tradition says that the founding chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hotu-matua">Hotu Matuʻa</a> landed there, and excavations show it was among the earliest settled places on the island, from around 1200. Its restored platforms, Ahu Nau Nau and Ahu Ature Huki, carry moai re-erected in the 20th century, the latter by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thor">Thor</a> Heyerdahl's expedition in 1956.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anakena.jpg" alt="Anakena" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bjørn Christian Tørrissen · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anakena-Ahu-Ature.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
